# Target C: a linear actual contact collar from diameter-leaf saturation

**Date:** 2026-10-02\
**Base main:** \`a5b22d70604c5da236f24587df43ac31a0d94e17\`\
**Status:** proof-only exact reduction. C-II and Level I remain open.

## 1. Setup

Work with the canonical post-ZF C-II source and its relative-interior full-support
KKT stress. Let

\[
H=(B,E_H)
\]

be the full actual diameter graph on the loaded terminal set \(B\), and write

\[
q=|B|,\qquad h=|E_H|,\qquad d_H=q-h,\qquad \nu=\nu(H).
\]

Let \(C\) be the full positive unit-contact graph. Write

\[
b
\]

for its outer-face boundary-walk length, counted with multiplicity, and

\[
x_B=b-q.
\]

Current main proves the exact defect identity

\[
\boxed{x_B+Q+2s_Z=h-d_H,}
\tag{1}
\]

with \(Q,s_Z\ge0\), and the new Woodall leaf/core theorem gives

\[
\boxed{
\ell_H:=|\{v\in B:\deg_H(v)=1\}|
\ge h-2\nu-1.
}
\tag{2}
\]

The point of this note is to convert (2) into a statement about **actual unit
contacts**, with no chart, stress floor, or small-\(x_B\) hypothesis.

## 2. Diameter leaves that are also contact leaves cost outer-walk excess

Put

\[
L_{11}:=\{v\in B:\deg_H(v)=1,\ \deg_C(v)=1\},
\qquad
\ell_{11}:=|L_{11}|.
\]

### Lemma 2.1

For every sufficiently large canonical source,

\[
\boxed{\ell_{11}\le x_B+1.}
\tag{3}
\]

### Proof

Every loaded terminal is a strict convex-hull vertex. A vertex \(v\in L_{11}\)
has exactly one unit-contact edge. That edge is a bridge of the connected positive
contact component and occurs twice on the outer-face boundary walk.

Two vertices of \(L_{11}\) cannot be adjacent to each other when \(D>2\). If they
were, their common contact edge would form a connected component consisting of that
single edge, because both endpoints have contact degree one. Canonical full-support
connectivity gives only one nontrivial positive contact component and makes it span
Euclidean diameter \(D\), impossible for a single unit edge.

Delete every vertex of \(L_{11}\) and its incident contact edge. The deleted edges
are distinct, and deleting pendant vertices preserves connectivity of the remaining
nonempty contact core. The outer boundary-walk length drops by exactly
\(2\ell_{11}\):

\[
b'=b-2\ell_{11}.
\tag{4}
\]

Every remaining loaded vertex is still extreme after deleting other source vertices,
so it still occurs on the outer face. Hence, with \(q'=q-\ell_{11}\),

\[
b'\ge q'-1.
\tag{5}
\]

The harmless minus one covers the degenerate final boundary case and is enough here.
Using (4),

\[
b-2\ell_{11}\ge q-\ell_{11}-1,
\]

so

\[
\ell_{11}\le b-q+1=x_B+1.
\]

QED.

### Exact geometry at a double leaf

If \(v\in L_{11}\), let \(a\) be its unique diameter partner and \(l\) its unique
contact neighbor. Relative-interior KKT positivity gives

\[
\alpha(v-l)=\beta(v-a),
\qquad
\alpha,\beta>0.
\]

Since \(|v-l|=1\) and \(|v-a|=D\),

\[
\boxed{
v-l={v-a\over D},\qquad
\alpha=D\beta,\qquad
|l-a|=D-1.
}
\tag{6}
\]

Thus the exceptional contact-leaf part of the collar consists of exact collinear
one-unit inward locks.

## 3. Count contact incidences at all diameter leaves

Let

\[
L_1:=\{v\in B:\deg_H(v)=1,\ \deg_C(v)=1\},
\]

\[
L_{\ge2}:=\{v\in B:\deg_H(v)=1,\ \deg_C(v)\ge2\}.
\]

Every loaded terminal has positive contact degree, so the diameter leaves split as

\[
\ell_H=|L_1|+|L_{\ge2}|.
\tag{7}
\]

Let \(E_C^{\rm leaf}\) be the set of distinct unit-contact edges having at least one
diameter-leaf endpoint.

The sum of contact degrees over all diameter leaves satisfies

\[
\sum_{\deg_H(v)=1}\deg_C(v)
\ge |L_1|+2|L_{\ge2}|
=2\ell_H-|L_1|.
\tag{8}
\]

One contact edge contributes to this sum at most twice. Therefore

\[
2|E_C^{\rm leaf}|
\ge 2\ell_H-|L_1|.
\tag{9}
\]

Use Lemma 2.1 and (2):

\[
|E_C^{\rm leaf}|
\ge
\ell_H-\frac{x_B+1}{2}
\ge
h-\frac{x_B}{2}-2\nu-\frac32.
\tag{10}
\]

In particular the integer-safe finite form is

\[
\boxed{
|E_C^{\rm leaf}|
\ge
h-\frac{x_B}{2}-2\nu-2.
}
\tag{11}
\]

This is already an exact bridge from diameter topology to actual unit-contact
support.

## 4. Eliminate the outer-walk variable

Equation (1) and nonnegativity give

\[
x_B\le h-d_H.
\tag{12}
\]

Insert (12) into (11):

\[
|E_C^{\rm leaf}|
\ge
h-\frac{h-d_H}{2}-2\nu-2
=
\frac{h+d_H}{2}-2\nu-2.
\]

Since \(q=h+d_H\),

\[
\boxed{
|E_C^{\rm leaf}|
\ge
\frac q2-2\nu-2.
}
\tag{13}
\]

Canonical ZF gives \(h\ge\lceil D\rceil\), and \(q\ge h\). Consequently

\[
\boxed{
|E_C^{\rm leaf}|
\ge
\frac{\lceil D\rceil}{2}-2\nu-2.
}
\tag{14}
\]

Thus every sublinear-matching sequence satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\nu=o(D)
\Longrightarrow
|E_C^{\rm leaf}|
\ge
\frac D2-o(D).
}
\tag{15}
\]

At the square-root hard scale,

\[
\nu=O(\sqrt D),
\]

one gets the finite-order version

\[
\boxed{
|E_C^{\rm leaf}|
\ge
\frac q2-O(\sqrt D)
\ge
\frac D2-O(\sqrt D).
}
\tag{16}
\]

No assumption on \(x_B/D\) appears.

## 5. Loaded-loaded versus loaded-unloaded collar

Every edge of \(E_C^{\rm leaf}\) has one loaded endpoint, namely a diameter leaf.
Its other endpoint is either loaded or unloaded. Split

\[
E_C^{\rm leaf}=E_{BB}^{\rm leaf}\sqcup E_{BI}^{\rm leaf}.
\]

Equation (13) gives the exact fork

\[
\boxed{
\max\{|E_{BB}^{\rm leaf}|,\ |E_{BI}^{\rm leaf}|\}
\ge
\frac q4-\nu-1.
}
\tag{17}
\]

Hence in every sublinear-matching sequence at least one of the following is present:

1. a linear family of loaded-loaded unit contacts incident with diameter leaves;
2. a linear family of loaded-unloaded attachment contacts incident with diameter
   leaves.

More precisely,

\[
\boxed{
\nu=o(D)
\Longrightarrow
\max\{|E_{BB}^{\rm leaf}|,\ |E_{BI}^{\rm leaf}|\}
\ge
\frac D4-o(D).
}
\tag{18}
\]

In the canonical full-core near-disk branch, every loaded-loaded unit contact is a
true loaded hull side. The second branch instead feeds directly into the harmonic
attachment-cell / loaded-boundary incidence system. Thus the hard diameter-matching
branch cannot avoid a linear physical contact interface by putting all its defect into
outer-walk repetitions.

## 6. Relation to the earlier leaf/KKT split

Current main already proves, for the same diameter-leaf population, the normalized
stress alternative

\[
\sum_{e\in C_{\rm leaf}}\alpha_e\ge D/4
\]

or a core diameter multiplier of order \(1/\nu\). The present theorem is independent:
it is a **support cardinality** statement, not a multiplier statement.

Together, at \(\nu=O(\sqrt D)\), the hard branch simultaneously has

\[
D-O(\sqrt D)
\]

diameter leaves on only \(O(\sqrt D)\) nonleaf diameter carriers,

\[
\frac D2-O(\sqrt D)
\]

distinct actual unit contacts touching those leaves, and either linear aggregate
leaf-contact stress or a \(D^{-1/2}\)-scale core diameter multiplier.

This is the first unconditional linear actual-contact collar obtained from the
square-root diameter branch; the earlier small-\(x_B\) corollary produced
\(D-o(D)\) contacts only after assuming \(x_B=o(D)\).

## 7. Remaining obstruction

Equation (18) identifies the next two concrete geometric cases.

- **Loaded-loaded collar:** exploit that these are hull sides in the full-core branch
  and couple their cyclic order to the contiguous Woodall diameter-star blocks.
- **Loaded-unloaded collar:** use attachment-cell planarity and the common
  2-edge-connected contact hub to bound how many diameter leaves one unloaded
  component can service without paying additional face/rank currency.

Neither multiplicity bound is proved here. The macroscopic inactive-container branch
remains independent.

**Disposition:** proof-only exact reduction. No computation, numerical experiment,
verifier execution, or CI output is used.

